@lesson robot-kinematics
Q: What is the inverse of a rotation matrix \(\mathbf R\)?
* \(\mathbf R^\top\)
- \(-\mathbf R\)
- \(\mathbf R^2\)
- It has no inverse
> Rotation matrices are orthogonal, so the transpose undoes the rotation.

Q: A two-link arm has \(l_1=l_2=1\), \(\theta_1=0\), \(\theta_2=90°\). What is the x-coordinate of the hand?
= 1 ±0.01
> \(x=\cos0+\cos(90°)=1+0=1\) (and \(y=0+\sin90°=1\)).

Q: Which rotation representation suffers from gimbal lock?
- Rotation matrices
* Euler angles
- Unit quaternions
- Axis–angle
> At certain angles two of the three Euler axes align and a degree of freedom is lost.

Q: Why does numerical inverse kinematics often use damped least squares?
* To stay stable near singularities where the Jacobian loses rank
- To make the arm move faster
- To avoid computing forward kinematics
- Because closed forms are always wrong
> The damping term \(\lambda^2\mathbf I\) keeps the inverse well-conditioned.

@lesson state-estimation
Q: The prediction variance is 4 and the sensor variance is 1. What is the 1-D Kalman gain?
= 0.8 ±0.001
> \(K=P^-/(P^-+R)=4/5\).

Q: After that update, what is the posterior variance?
= 0.8 ±0.001
> \(P=P^-R/(P^-+R)=4/5\): lower than both sources.

Q: Which filter can represent a belief with two separate peaks?
- Kalman filter
- Extended Kalman filter
* Particle filter
- A single Gaussian
> Weighted samples can represent any shape of distribution.

Q: In the Kalman filter, the prediction step increases uncertainty and the measurement update decreases it.
= true
> Motion adds noise \(\mathbf Q\); measurements add information.

@lesson robot-control
Q: Which PID term removes steady-state error against a constant disturbance like gravity?
- Proportional
* Integral
- Derivative
- None
> The integral accumulates the remaining error until the output cancels the disturbance.

Q: Is \(x_{t+1}=1.2x_t+u_t\) with feedback \(u_t=-0.5x_t\) stable?
= true
> The closed loop is \(x_{t+1}=0.7x_t\) and \(|0.7|\lt1\).

Q: What does model predictive control do at every time step?
* Optimizes a short-horizon control sequence under constraints and applies only the first action
- Applies a fixed gain forever
- Trains a neural network
- Waits for the error to grow
> Re-planning with each new measurement handles disturbances and constraints.

Q: In LQR, increasing the control cost matrix \(\mathbf R\) makes the controller…
* gentler, using less effort and tracking more slowly
- more aggressive
- unstable
- ignore the state
> \(\mathbf R\) penalizes large control inputs.

@lesson motion-planning
Q: When is A* guaranteed to find the shortest path?
* When its heuristic never overestimates the remaining cost (admissible)
- Always, with any heuristic
- Only on graphs with no obstacles
- Only with a zero heuristic
> With \(h=0\) A* becomes Dijkstra, which is also optimal but slower.

Q: Why are sampling-based planners like RRT preferred for 7-joint arms?
* Grids grow exponentially with dimension, while sampling only explores where it goes
- RRT always finds the optimal path
- Grids cannot represent obstacles
- RRT needs no collision checking
> RRT is probabilistically complete; RRT* also converges toward optimal.

Q: What does RRT* add to RRT?
- A heuristic function
* Rewiring nearby nodes to cheaper parents so paths converge toward optimal
- A grid
- A neural network
> Plain RRT finds feasible but jagged, suboptimal paths.

@lesson robot-learning
Q: What is domain randomization?
* Randomizing simulator physics and visuals so a policy works across many variations, including reality
- Randomly choosing which robot to use
- Adding noise to rewards
- Training on random labels
> If the real world falls inside the training range, the policy transfers.

Q: Why do diffusion policies help imitation learning from human demonstrations?
* They model multi-modal action distributions instead of averaging modes
- They are faster than MLPs
- They need no data
- They remove the need for cameras
> Averaging "go left" and "go right" with MSE produces an invalid "go straight".

Q: What does a vision-language-action model output?
- Captions
* Robot actions conditioned on images and an instruction
- Depth maps only
- Reward values
> VLAs start from vision-language models and are fine-tuned on robot data.

@lesson exam
Q: A robot localizes with GPS (noise std 3 m) and wheel odometry that drifts. Which approach fuses them best?
* A Kalman filter (or EKF) that predicts with odometry and updates with GPS
- Averaging the two positions
- Using only GPS
- Using only odometry
> The filter weights each source by its uncertainty over time.

Q: Order the classical stack: perception and estimation, planning, control. Which runs at the highest frequency?
- Route planning
- Behaviour planning
* Low-level control
- Map building
> Control loops often run at hundreds or thousands of hertz; planners much more slowly.

Q: A learned locomotion policy works perfectly in simulation and falls over on the real robot. Which techniques address this? (select all)
* Domain randomization
* System identification of real parameters
* A privileged teacher distilled into a sensor-only student
- Training longer in the same simulator settings
> These are the standard sim-to-real tools.

Q: A drone's altitude oscillates more and more after a gain change. What is the most likely cause?
* The proportional (or integral) gain is too high, making the loop unstable
- The derivative gain is too high
- The sensor is perfect
- The target is too low
> Too much gain pushes closed-loop poles outside the stable region.
